// @no-instantiate
// MISCOMPILE, pinned: `std:array`'s generic exports over a LITERAL-UNION element emit
// a module the engine refuses. This is the worst of the three answers `std:array`'s
// generic surface gives (the module header enumerates them), and it is worth a fixture
// precisely because it is NOT an error: `vl check` exits 0 with no diagnostic at any
// severity, `vl build` writes the `.wasm` file and reports success, and the failure
// arrives only when something tries to instantiate it —
//
//   type mismatch: expected (ref $type), found i32
//
// PRE-EXISTING, and not `sorted`'s doing. The same program with `indexOf`,
// `lastIndexOf`, `includes`, `count`, `reduce` or `mapIndexed` produces the identical
// invalid module, as does a hand-written `function pick<T>(self: T[], f: (T, T) =>
// boolean)` with no std involved. The dividing line is the SIGNATURE: `reverse` and
// `concat` mention nothing but `T` and `T[]` and handle a string literal union
// correctly; every export that puts `T` in a CLOSURE parameter or compares two `T`s
// with `==` does not. A NUMERIC literal union (`(1 | 2)[]`) is refused earlier, at
// emit, even by `reverse`.
//
// HOW IT GOT INTO THE HEADER AS "WORKS". The reach paragraph in `std/array.vl` first
// claimed literal unions were admitted, on the strength of a probe that used
// `reverse` — the one shape-blind export in the module. Probing a generic surface with
// its most permissive arm measures the arm, not the surface; the header now says so
// and this file is the witness.
//
// WHEN IT IS FIXED, this file starts failing (the module instantiates), which is the
// point. The fix is to delete it and fold the literal-union case into
// `array-sorted.vl` beside the `string` receiver, and to correct the header's
// enumeration.
import { sorted } from "std:array"

const tags: ("a" | "b")[] = ["b", "a", "b"]
print(tags.sorted((x: "a" | "b", y: "a" | "b") => x < y)[0])
